## How to Improve This Paper

### Missing Experiments (top 3-5 only)
1. **Quantitative robustness evaluation with noisy/missing 2D joints is absent.** The paper's title and abstract prominently claim robustness to joint noise, but only qualitative visual comparisons (Figure 1b, 5) are provided. Add experiments with varying levels of Gaussian noise added to 2D detections and with random joint dropout at different percentages, reporting MPJPE curves — without this, the robustness claim is unsubstantiated.

2. **No comparison with efficient/lightweight baselines under matched conditions.** The paper compares against heavy models (MixSTE, KTPFormer) to claim efficiency gains. Include comparisons with methods specifically designed for efficiency (e.g., P-STMO, VideoPose3D with shorter sequences, or simple downsampling baselines) at comparable parameter/FLOP budgets to validate the efficiency-accuracy tradeoff claim is non-trivial.

3. **No ablation over the choice of 5 body parts or alternative groupings.** The Part-based Adaptive GNN is a core contribution, but the number and definition of body parts (5 groups) appears arbitrary. Ablate with different part configurations (e.g., 3, 7, or limb-wise vs. torso+limbs grouping) to show this design is principled rather than hand-tuned.

4. **No measured inference latency, only FLOPs.** The "~1% computational cost" claim rests on FLOPs, but FLOPs don't always correlate with wall-clock speed due to parallelism differences. Report actual GPU/CPU inference time to substantiate the practical efficiency claim.

### Deeper Analysis Needed (top 3-5 only)
1. **Analysis of the learned spatial graph topology.** The paper claims "fully adaptive" topology but never shows what connections are actually learned or whether they correspond to physically meaningful part interactions. Visualize learned adjacency matrices across actions and training stages; otherwise, the claim that part-based adaptive graph captures meaningful coordination is unverified.

2. **Breakdown of computational cost by module.** Without knowing how much of the efficiency gain comes from the Part-based GNN (spatial compression) vs. Skipped Transformer (temporal compression), it's impossible to attribute the improvements. Provide FLOPs/params per module.

