2. **The wavelet domain's high-frequency coefficient sparsity and its incompatibility with Gaussian diffusion assumptions are insufficiently discussed**: Performing diffusion in the wavelet domain is the core methodological contribution, but wavelet high-frequency coefficients are known to be sparse and non-Gaussian, which fundamentally conflicts with the Gaussian noise assumptions of DDPM. While this paper applies diffusion uniformly on all wavelet coefficients, prior work on wavelet-domain generative modeling has explicitly flagged this issue: "within the wavelet domain, we encounter unique complexities, especially the sparse representation of high-frequency coefficients, which deviates significantly from the Gaussian assumptions in the diffusion process" (from the Multi-Scale Generative Modeling in Wavelet Domain review, `/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/pXEnurdRAx.md`). The paper does not analyze or even acknowledge how this distributional mismatch affects the quality of generation, and no empirical analysis of the wavelet coefficient distributions is provided.

